# deploy/crontab.stale-member-alert — the schedule of record for the dark-host
# operator sweep (t_e5dcc325, 2026-11-27).
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# ``find_stale_members`` shipped in fleetos_1607 Phase A as the documented
# replacement for the deleted Phase F failover — the thing whose operator
# outcome is "a host went dark, do something" — and had ZERO callers in app/.
# Only tests imported it. It stayed that way from 2026-07-16 to 2026-11-27
# while prod carried ``astrovita``: is_active=true since 2026-08-05 with no
# fleet_member_liveness row at all, i.e. a host dark for 114 days that a
# correct, unit-tested, never-invoked detector silently failed to report. An
# uninstalled schedule is not a schedule, and an uninvoked detector is not an
# alert — which is precisely this card's title. See the identical diagnosis in
# app/crons/sync_report_pruner.py (prune_raw: 4.5 months, no caller).
#
# The live mechanism on this host is the `wisechef` user crontab, so that is
# where this job lives, and the line below is kept byte-identical to
# CRONTAB_LINE in app/crons/stale_member_alert.py —
# tests/test_t_e5dcc325_stale_member_caller.py asserts they match, so code and
# schedule cannot drift apart silently.
#
# EXIT CODES (part of the interface, asserted by tests)
#   0 = sweep ran, saw >=1 active member, nothing dark
#   1 = the sweeper itself failed
#   2 = refused: the sweep saw ZERO active members (typo'd fleet, wrong .env)
#       — "I could not see anything" is not an all-clear and must not exit 0
#   3 = usage error, via app.crons._cli.Parser (a typo'd flag, not a verdict)
#   4 = lock conflict (flock -E 4) — a {"skipped": ...} marker line is written,
#       never a silent exit 1 masquerading as a crash (app/crons/_cli.py contract)
#   5 = sweep ran and FOUND dark hosts  <- grep this, not "did it crash"
# 5 is deliberately outside the package contract 0-4 (3 is usage, 4 is the
# reserved flock-conflict code) and distinct from 1, so a crashed sweeper can
# never read as an all-clear. That conflation is how the previous alert died:
# quietly.
#
# INSTALL (idempotent — installs the line from the module, not from here):
#   cd /home/wisechef/loopskill-api && set -a; . ./.env; set +a
#   LINE=$(venv/bin/python -c 'from app.crons.stale_member_alert import CRONTAB_LINE; print(CRONTAB_LINE)')
#   crontab -l 2>/dev/null | grep -v stale_member_alert > /tmp/ct.new
#   echo "$LINE" >> /tmp/ct.new && crontab /tmp/ct.new
# VERIFY it is installed and actually running:
#   crontab -l | grep stale_member_alert
#   tail -5 /home/wisechef/wiserecipes-logs/stale_member_alert.log
# and the standing predicate ~/.hermes/goals/fleet-stale-member-alert.md
# (daily verify-goals.sh sweep), which asserts the sweep ran in the last 26h AND
# that every active member with a current fleet_members.updated_at also has a
# liveness row — the latter is what proves the detector is still wired, not just
# that a cron line exists.

15 * * * * cd /home/wisechef/loopskill-api && set -a; . ./.env; set +a; flock -n -E 4 /tmp/loopskill-stale-member-alert.lock venv/bin/python -m app.crons.stale_member_alert --json >> /home/wisechef/wiserecipes-logs/stale_member_alert.log 2>&1; rc=$?; [ $rc -ne 4 ] || echo '{"skipped": "lock held by a live run"}' >> /home/wisechef/wiserecipes-logs/stale_member_alert.log 2>&1; exit $rc
